## Test-running discipline

Do **not** run the full pytest suite reflexively. Tests are slow and waste session time. Run them only when:

- The change you just made plausibly affects test outcomes (touched `src/`, `tests/`, or a spec a conformance vector pins).
- The user explicitly asks for a test run.
- You are gating a destructive operation (rebase, force-push, schema migration) where regression detection is the whole point.

For documentation-only edits, frontmatter changes, or work confined to `library/`, `operations/command/`, `operations/engineering/skills/`, etc., skip pytest entirely.

## Curation discipline

The library's curation discipline is itself a *theory* in the corpus. Read it as such before touching library content:

- **The theory** — `library/theories/science/theories/information/theories/knowledge-systems/theories/markdown-vault-knowledge-system/theories/predicated-markdown-vault-knowledge-system/_index.md`. Built from the markdown-vault commitments and the predicated-knowledge-system commitments; depends on [predication-categories](library/theories/humanities/theories/math/theories/categories/theories/predication-categories/_index.md) and [predicated-computing](library/theories/engineering/theories/tech/theories/computing/theories/predicated-computing/_index.md) as gold-standard precedents.
- **The questions** — `library/.../predicated-markdown-vault-knowledge-system/questions/`. Read these *first*; they frame what the conventions are trying to settle.
- **The current draft answers** — `library/.../predicated-markdown-vault-knowledge-system/specifications/`. Per-type specs and cross-cutting commitments, currently working-draft.

